{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 8, "model_name": "nvidia/llama-3.3-nemotron-super-49b-v1", "content": "Here is a 300+ word summary of the Wikipedia page \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raymond_III,_Count_of_Tripoli\" without commas and with at least 3 highlighted sections in markdown format:\n\nRaymond III Count of Tripoli was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Helena of Armenia\nHe succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1153 after Raymond II was murdered\nRaymond III was only a child when he ascended to the throne and his mother Helena acted as regent until he came of age\n\n*Raymond III played a crucial role in the politics of the Crusader states often finding himself at odds with King Amalric I of Jerusalem*\n\nHe was taken hostage by the Muslims in 1163 during a battle near Tripoli and was released in 1164 after a ransom was paid\nRaymond III then formed an alliance with the Byzantine Empire through his marriage to Maria of Antioch\nThis alliance was short lived as Raymond III soon found himself in conflict with the Byzantines\n\n*The County of Tripoli under Raymond III suffered greatly during the reign of Saladin who repeatedly attacked the region*\n\nRaymond III was present at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader forces were heavily defeated by Saladin\nHe fled to Tripoli after the battle and played a key role in the defense of the city against Saladin's forces\nRaymond III died in 1188 at the age of 45 and was succeeded by his nephew Henry I Count of Champagne\n\n*Raymond III's legacy is often overshadowed by other Crusader leaders despite his significant contributions to the history of the Crusader states* \n\nRaymond III's death marked the end of the direct line of Counts of Tripoli from the House of Flanders\nHis reign was marked by constant conflict with the Muslim states and internal power struggles within the Crusader kingdoms\nDespite these challenges Raymond III managed to maintain the independence of t